<article class="article-body">

<p>When a team's response times are slow, the instinct is usually to tell people to move faster. That almost never works, because slow response is rarely a speed problem. It's a visibility problem: nobody can see what's waiting, who owns it, or how long it's been sitting, so things drift until someone escalates. Across the teams I've worked with, the fix that actually moves the number, 80% faster response in one recent engagement, has never been "try harder." It's been making the backlog visible and giving it an owner.</p>

<h2>You can't fix what isn't visible</h2>
<p>Before any system change, most teams can't actually answer a simple question: how many open items exist right now, and how old is the oldest one? Email and chat hide this by design, everything looks equally urgent because nothing is sorted by age or owner. The first move is always a dashboard, not a pep talk: a single view of every open item, how long it's been open, and whose name is on it.</p>

<h2>SLA tracking turns "soon" into a number</h2>
<p>"We'll get back to you soon" is not a commitment anyone can be held to. An SLA, even an internal one nobody outside the company ever sees, gives a response time a number: four hours, one business day, whatever's realistic for that queue. Once there's a number, there's something to measure against, and a dashboard that shows items breaching that number before a customer has to ask where their reply is.</p>

<h2>A decision log stops the same question from being asked twice</h2>
<p>A huge share of slow response isn't about the first reply, it's about someone needing a decision that was already made somewhere else and forgotten. A decision log, a simple running record of what was decided, when, and by whom, means the person answering a ticket doesn't have to track down whoever made the original call. It also means decisions survive someone being on leave or leaving the company entirely.</p>

<h2>The weekly cadence is what makes it stick</h2>
<p>None of this holds without a recurring check-in: a short weekly review of what's breaching SLA, what's stuck, and what needs to change about the process itself. Without that cadence, dashboards quietly stop being updated within a month. With it, the system catches its own decay before it becomes invisible again.</p>

<h2>What this actually looks like, built</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>A live dashboard</strong> showing every open item, its age, and its owner, visible to the whole team, not just a manager.</li>
  <li><strong>SLA targets with real numbers</strong>, tracked automatically where possible, flagged manually where it isn't.</li>
  <li><strong>A decision log</strong> that's actually searchable, not a buried email thread.</li>
  <li><strong>A standing weekly review</strong> that looks at the system itself, not just the backlog.</li>
</ul>

